hi Claude. Here in /Users/jeff/go/src/github.com/gmofishsauce/SpaceGame
there is a game, implemented as a  single page application, provided
by a small server written in Golang. The server offers  APIs so it
can act as the authoritative source of game information. The SPA
is implemented entirely in JavaScript.

The game is a slow moving model of interstellar warfare with speed
of light delays playing a key role. The intent is that the players
view of the entire star cluster (battle area) is based on the
assumption of actions and state updates that are greatly delayed
when viewed from Sol's frame of reference.  effect. The displayed
state resulting from those actions may be corrected to reality later
when new, lightspeed-delayed information arrives at Sol.

Today's goal is the architectural review and discussion described below.

Note that I said "players", plural. This is a two player game, but
the design is currently asymmetrical: there is a human player who
sees state derived from an API, and an omniscient, built-in bot
that forms the opponent for the human player.

I would like to investigate making the game symmetrical. There would
still be a bot in the symmetrical game, but the bot would be forced
to play against the same kind of light speed-delayed information seen by
the human player. This would require many conceptual changes in
addition to the major code change that is implied. The bot player
would need its own home star playing the same role that Sol plays
for the human. The bot player would also require a collection of
stars near near the designated home star, each with their own economy--
just like the human player has a set of star systems surrounding
its home star at game start.

For now please assume that the alien home star is 61 Ursae Majoris
(61 UMa) and that this star has planets (in fact, none are known).
Further assume the existence of CSV files similar to nearest.csv
and planets.csv defining a collection of populated systems for the
alien player. The contents of these files would be assembled as a
task separate from the changes to the game itself.

In this new design, the server will have to accept connections from
up to two client programs. You may assume that the server will only
accept local connections, so security is not an issue for now. The
two local connections would be from a local browser for the human
player and a newly designed stadnalone  bot that plays against the
API interface. All places in the code that support the existing
omniscient bot player would be cleaned up and removed.

